Where, after bathing, the ailment is cured by the effect of the curse. Vimuktah kilbishatsadyo, yeh bhutah kalodayam. 36. Meaning of the word

Where—where; after bathing; Daksha-shapat—from the curse of Prajapati Daksha; Grihitah—caught, afflicted; Yakshmana—of lung diseases; Yakshma—of lung disease; Udu-rat—the king of stars, the moon; Vimuktah—freed; Kilbishat—from one's sins; Sadyah—immediately;
Bheje—held; Bhuyah—again; Kala—of one's arts; Udayam—increase. Once, due to Daksha's curse, the moon was afflicted with tuberculosis, but by merely bathing in the Prabhas region,
the moon was immediately freed from all its sins, and it also attained the
growth of its arts. And having offered water to the ancestors. Bhojayitvoshijo vipranananagunavatandhasa. 37). With those grains, in the vessels, with faith, greatly.
I will cross over, like a boat across a flood, with charity. 37). Meaning of the word:

Vayam—We; Cha—also; Tasmin—in that place; Aplutya—after bathing; Tarpitva—by offering libations, by satisfying;
Pitrun—to the ancestors; Suran—as well as the gods; Bhojayitva—by feeding; Ushijah—venerable; Vipran—to the Brahmins;
Nana—various; Guna-vata—of good qualities; Anandasah—from food; Teshu—in them; Daanani—of donations, Offerings; vessels—of charity;
worthy person; with faith—with reverence; having become—great; Vai—indeed; Vrijinaani—crisis;
Tarishyamah—we will be able to cross; Daanaih—with your charity; Naubhih—by boats; Iva—like; Aranvam—the ocean.
To bathe in the Prabhas region, to please the ancestors and gods there by offering sacrifices,
to feed the venerable Brahmins various delicious dishes and considering them worthy of donation,
by giving sufficient donations, we can surely overcome these terrible difficulties, just as a man
can cross the ocean by boarding a suitable boat. Srisuka said:

Thus, the Yadavas, having been directed by the Lord, performed the rituals. Gunthum krutadhiyastirtham syandanansamyujan. 31. Meaning of the word

Shri-Shuka said—Shri Shukadeva Goswami said; Evam—In this way; Bhagwata—By the Lord; Adishtaha—Order given;
Yadavah—The Yadavas; Kuru-Nandana—Dear to the Kurus; Gantum—To go; Krita-Dhiyaha—With resolve in mind; Tirtham—A holy place; Syandanan—In their chariots; Samayuyujjan—Horses yoked.
